recover: recover

recover returns the panic value only when it is called directly by a deferred function while
the program is panicking. C++ has no such rule, so a function that calls recover is "aware":
it takes a ticket at its entry (gp::defer_ctx gp_dc = gp::take_defer_ctx()), which says
whether a deferred call of a panicking function is running it. gp::recover(gp_dc) then
returns the panic and stops it, or nil.

The defer machinery calls an aware function differently from a plain one: gp_push_aware for
a function of the program, gp_push_call_aware for a func literal, gp_push_call_icall for a
method called through an interface (the runtime looks at the method's thunk then). A func
value is looked up when it is called: a literal that contains recover is made as
gp::func<Sig>(gp::aware(lambda)).

A function without recover is written exactly as it would be without this feature.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#defer
See also: gocpp explain defer, gocpp explain panics
Also known as: aware

Example (recover.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    func safeDiv(a, b int) (q int, err error) {
    	defer func() {
    		if r := recover(); r != nil {
    			err = fmt.Errorf("recovered: %v", r)
    		}
    	}()
    	return a / b, nil
    }

    func main() {
    	fmt.Println(safeDiv(7, 2))
    	fmt.Println(safeDiv(1, 0))
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    std::tuple<int64_t, gp::error> safeDiv(int64_t a, int64_t b);
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/recover.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/recover.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./recover.gp:5: func safeDiv(a, b int) (q int, err error) {
    // gp[defer] col 1: this function has a defer: the body runs in try { } with a gp::defer_frame
    //     (gp_df); every exit goes through gp_df.gp_run(), which runs the deferred calls last in, first
    //     out, then rethrows a pending panic; the results are variables so deferred closures can change
    //     them
    // gp[boxing] col 32: err is boxed: a closure captures it and it changes, so the function and the
    //     closure share one box (a copy would miss the changes)
    std::tuple<int64_t, gp::error> safeDiv(int64_t a, int64_t b) {
        int64_t q{};
        gp::ptr<gp::error> err = gp::new_<gp::error>();
        gp::defer_frame gp_df; try {

    // ---- ./recover.gp:6: defer func() {
    // gp[defer] col 2: defer: the function and its arguments are evaluated now and stored in the frame;
    //     the call runs when the function exits: a func literal: its lambda goes into the frame
    //     (gp_push_call), so the stack trace names it func1, func2...
    // gp[closures] col 8: func literal #1 is a C++ lambda wrapped in gp::func (the stack trace names it
    //     func1); copies of the func value share the captured state
    // gp[recover] col 8: this function calls recover(): it takes a ticket (gp::defer_ctx gp_dc) at
    //     entry; recover returns the panic value only when a deferred call of a panicking function
    //     calls it directly, as in Go
            gp_df.gp_push_call_aware([err]<int = 1>() mutable -> void {
                gp::defer_ctx gp_dc = gp::take_defer_ctx();

    // ---- ./recover.gp:7: if r := recover(); r != nil {
    // gp[recover] col 11: recover(): gp::recover(gp_dc) takes the pending panic with this function's
    //     ticket, only if a deferred call of a panicking function is the caller; else it returns nil
                {
                    gp::any r = gp::recover(gp_dc);
                    if (r != nullptr) {

    // ---- ./recover.gp:8: err = fmt.Errorf("recovered: %v", r)
    // gp[closures] col 4: captures err by sharing its box (it changes after capture)
    // gp[interfaces] col 10: fmt.Errorf: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
                        (*err) = gp::fmt::Errorf("recovered: %v"_gs, r);

    // ---- ./recover.gp:9: }
                    }
                }

    // ---- ./recover.gp:10: }()
            }

    // ---- ./recover.gp:6: defer func() {  (continued)
            );

    // ---- ./recover.gp:11: return a / b, nil
    // gp[defer] col 2: return assigns the results first, then jumps to gp_ret0, which runs the deferred
    //     calls and returns the results (Go's order)
    // gp[checks] col 11: division of integers: gp::div panics for a zero divisor and gives Go's result
    //     for MIN / -1; these checks stay under -nochecks
            { auto gp_t0 = gp::error(); q = gp::div(a, b); (*err) = gp_t0; } goto gp_ret0;

    // ---- ./recover.gp:12: }
        } catch (...) {
            gp_df.gp_on_exception();
        }
        gp_df.gp_run(); return {q, (*err)};

    // ---- ./recover.gp:11: return a / b, nil  (continued)
        gp_ret0:; gp_df.gp_run(); return {q, (*err)};

    // ---- ./recover.gp:12: }  (continued)
    }

    // ---- ./recover.gp:14: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./recover.gp:15: fmt.Println(safeDiv(7, 2))
    // gp[interfaces] col 2: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
        std::apply([](auto&& gp_r0, auto&& gp_r1) { return gp::fmt::Println(gp_r0, gp_r1); }, safeDiv(7, 2));

    // ---- ./recover.gp:16: fmt.Println(safeDiv(1, 0))
        std::apply([](auto&& gp_r0, auto&& gp_r1) { return gp::fmt::Println(gp_r0, gp_r1); }, safeDiv(1, 0));

    // ---- ./recover.gp:17: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
